---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PlatformDiagram from '../components/PlatformDiagram.astro';
import HeroDiagram from '../components/HeroDiagram.astro';
import { CHAT_OPTIONS } from '../scripts/chat-options';
import { CASE_OPTIONS } from '../scripts/case-options';
import '../styles/home.css';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="DrakeWorks"
	description="Drake is a product builder who owns products from the first spec to the last edge case: platforms, APIs, payments and wallets, from zero to scale."
	viewport="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover, interactive-widget=resizes-content"
>
	<Fragment slot="head">
		<meta name="theme-color" content="#0A0B0D" />
		<meta name="color-scheme" content="dark" />
		<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
		<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
		<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:ital,opsz,wght@0,14..32,400;0,14..32,500;0,14..32,600;0,14..32,700;1,14..32,600&family=Geist+Mono:wght@400;500&family=Caveat:wght@600&display=swap" />
	</Fragment>
{/* ============ NAV ============ */}
<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
<header class="nav">
  <div class="w nin"><a class="brand" href="#home" data-go="home"><svg class="logo" viewBox="8 2 88 88" aria-hidden="true"><circle class="ld" cx="28" cy="62" r="16"/><path class="ld" d="M44 14V78"/><path class="lw" d="M54 48L62 76L72 52L82 76L92 48"/></svg><span>DrakeWorks</span></a>
    <nav id="anav" class="links" aria-label="Primary" data-nav><a href="#work" data-go="home" data-anchor="work" data-work>Work</a><a href="#approach" data-go="home" data-anchor="approach">Approach</a><a href="#about" data-go="about">About</a></nav>
    <div class="nr"><button class="kbtn" data-cmdk-open aria-label="Open command menu"><span>Search</span><kbd>⌘K</kbd></button><a class="btn sm" href="#contact" data-go="home" data-anchor="contact">Contact</a><button class="menu-btn" data-menu aria-controls="anav" aria-expanded="false">Menu</button></div>
  </div>
</header>
<main id="main">
  {/* ============ HOME ============ */}
  <div data-view="home">
    {/* 1. Hero: the headline and introduction beside a rotating diagram of the six areas I work in
         (HeroDiagram.astro, driven by hero-rotator.js). Grid areas put it beside the copy on desktop,
         and between the headline and the introduction on phones. */}
    <section class="w hero hr">
      <div class="hr-head">
        <h1><span class="ln grad">Zero to one creates possibility.</span> <span class="ln grad dim">One to 100 makes an impact.</span></h1>
      </div>
      <figure class="hr-fig">
        <HeroDiagram />
      </figure>
      <div class="hr-intro">
        <p class="hr-hi">Hi, I’m Drake. <span aria-hidden="true">👋</span></p>
        <p class="hr-role">I’m a product builder.</p>
        <p class="hr-sub">I own products from the first spec to the last edge case.</p>
        <div class="ctas"><a class="btn" href="#work" data-go="home" data-anchor="work">Explore my work</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#" data-link="linkedin" data-cta="Opens Drake’s LinkedIn profile.">LinkedIn</a></div>
      </div>
    </section>
    {/* Numbers: the proof, straight after the hero */}
    <section class="w nums" aria-label="Proof points">
      <ul>
        <li><b>US$1B+</b><span>a year through the platform I built</span></li>
        <li><b>−<span data-to="68">68</span>%</b><span>payment costs in the gateway’s first year</span></li>
        <li><b>4 → <span data-from="4" data-to="14">14</span></b><span>from 4 engineers I hired to a team of 14</span></li>
        <li><b>&lt;1 yr</b><span>from first sketch to live gateway</span></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    {/* 2. Career: heading and intro pinned in a side column (CI03), with the timeline rail (C02)
         beside it. One dot per role, newest first; the rail turns accent where product starts (pd),
         fades back at the last product role (pd-end), and the current role's dot (on) pulses. */}
    <section class="w sec career">
      <div class="car-head">
        <h2 class="grad">Career</h2>
        <p>I started out testing games and recruiting the people who made them. Then came project management, then product. <strong>Product&nbsp;stuck.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <ol class="log">
        <li class="pd on"><time>2026–now</time>
          <div><b>Senior Product Manager</b><span>sFOX</span>
          <p>Building sFOX Connect, a B2B2C API product, and onboarding and compliance for the sFOX Direct platform.</p></div>
        </li>
        <li class="pd pd-end"><time>2021–2026</time>
          <div><b>Senior Product Manager (Head of Blockchain)</b><span>OG Global (Funpodium &amp; GmindTech)</span>
          <p>Built and ran the blockchain payments division: owned the roadmap, budget and vendor decisions, hired the team (4 → 14) and reported to the CTO. Shipped a gateway, a wallet, a treasury product and a 40+ provider orchestrator.</p></div>
        </li>
        <li><time>2018–2020</time>
          <div><b>Senior Technical Project Manager</b><span>OG Global</span>
          <p>Built the project and product team at a new startup in the group with the CEO, and moved outsourced projects to a new in-house engineering team.</p></div>
        </li>
        <li><time>2015–2018</time>
          <div><b>Project Manager</b><span>LF Consulting</span>
          <p>Delivered software projects for e-commerce and video game industry clients.</p></div>
        </li>
        <li><time>2009–2014</time>
          <div><b>Quality Assurance Analyst, then Senior Recruiting Specialist</b><span>Electronic Arts</span>
          <p>Helped ship AAA games at one of the world’s largest video game publishers, first in QA, then in recruiting.</p></div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>
    {/* 4. What I’ve built */}
    <section class="w sec" id="work">
      <div class="sh">
        <h2 class="grad">What I’ve built</h2>
        <p>What I decided, what I traded off and what shipped.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cards">
        {/* The flagship gets the full width, with two of its screens: a gateway deposit and treasury approvals */}
        <a class="card flag" data-spot href="#case-1" data-go="case-1">
          <div class="cv">
            <div class="mk" aria-hidden="true">
              <div class="mk-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>gateway — incoming deposit</span></div>
              <div class="mk-ap">
                <div class="mk-head"><b>Deposit</b><span class="mk-chip">Two callbacks</span><span class="mk-fil">Every step logged</span></div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">1</span>
                  <div><b>Seen on the network</b><span>First callback <i class="">Sent</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Sent</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">2</span>
                  <div><b>Confirmed</b><span>Sender address screened <i class="">Clean</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Pass</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">3</span>
                  <div><b>Limits and fee</b><span>Within limits <i class="">Fee applied</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Pass</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">4</span>
                  <div><b>Credited</b><span>Clean balance <i class="">Final callback</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Done</span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="mk" aria-hidden="true">
              <div class="mk-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>treasury — payouts and approvals</span></div>
              <div class="mk-ap">
                <div class="mk-head"><b>Waiting for approval</b><span class="mk-chip">Up to 3 layers</span><span class="mk-fil">Every action logged</span></div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">P</span>
                  <div><b>Supplier payout</b><span>Approval 2 of 3 <i class="">Address screened</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Approve</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">S</span>
                  <div><b>Client settlement</b><span>Maker-checker <i class="">Wallet registered</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Approve</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">W</span>
                  <div><b>Customer withdrawal</b><span>Paid from deposit address <i class="">Routed</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Sent</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">D</span>
                  <div><b>Incoming deposit</b><span>Higher-risk source <i class="warn">Isolated for review</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Review</span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="ct"><span class="tag">Build and scale</span>
            <h3>From a one-line brief to a payments platform</h3>
            <p class="res"><span>Payment costs: <b>−68%</b></span> · <span>Clients: <b>2 → <span aria-hidden="true">~</span><span class="sr">about </span>20</b></span> · <span>Volume: <b>Zero → $1B+</b></span></p>
          </div>
        </a>
        {CASE_OPTIONS.walletCase && (
        <a class="card slim" data-spot href="#case-3" data-go="case-3">
          {/* The same wallet on web and mobile (they launched together), the phone in front */}
          <div class="cv wl">
            <div class="wl-g" aria-hidden="true">
              <div class="mk wl-w">
                <div class="mk-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>wallet — web</span></div>
                <div class="wl-b">
                  <ul class="wl-side">
                    <li class="mk-ws"><b>W</b>Wallet</li>
                    <li class="on">Overview</li><li>Send and receive</li><li>Swap</li><li>Activity</li>
                  </ul>
                  <div class="mk-main pad">
                    <div class="wl-tot"><span>Total balance</span><b>$12,480.20</b><span class="mk-chip">5 networks</span></div>
                    <div class="wl-acts"><span class="on">Send</span><span>Receive</span><span>Swap</span></div>
                    <p class="mk-sm">Recent activity</p>
                    <div class="wl-tx"><i style="--c:var(--mk-ok)">↓</i><div><b>Received USDC</b><span>Ethereum · address screened</span></div><code>+250.00</code></div>
                    <div class="wl-tx"><i style="--c:var(--mk-acc2)">⇄</i><div><b>Swapped USDC</b><span>USDC → ETH</span></div><code>+0.10</code></div>
                    <div class="wl-tx"><i style="--c:var(--mk-acc)">↑</i><div><b>Sent SOL</b><span>Solana · to a saved address</span></div><code>−2.00</code></div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="wl-ph"><div class="wl-s">
                <div class="wl-sb"><b>9:41</b><span class="wl-sig"><i></i><i></i><i></i><u></u></span></div>
                <div class="wl-hd"><b>Wallet</b><span class="mk-av">DK</span></div>
                <div class="wl-bal"><span>Total balance</span><b>$12,480.20</b></div>
                <div class="wl-pa"><span><i class="on">↑</i>Send</span><span><i>↓</i>Receive</span><span><i>⇄</i>Swap</span></div>
                <div class="wl-coin"><i style="--c:var(--mk-warn)">B</i><div><b>BTC</b><small>Bitcoin</small></div><code>0.042</code></div>
                <div class="wl-coin"><i style="--c:var(--mk-acc)">E</i><div><b>ETH</b><small>Ethereum</small></div><code>1.20</code></div>
                <div class="wl-coin"><i style="--c:var(--mk-acc2)">S</i><div><b>SOL</b><small>Solana</small></div><code>18.5</code></div>
                <div class="wl-coin"><i style="--c:#4A90E2">U</i><div><b>USDC</b><small>ERC-20</small></div><code>2,500</code></div>
              </div></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="ct"><span class="tag">Customer-led</span>
            <h3>A turnkey wallet, built on our own gateway</h3>
            <p class="res"><span>Clients: <b>1 → 3</b></span> · <span>Identity checks: <b>24–48 h → instant</b></span></p>
          </div>
        </a>
        )}
        <a class="card slim" data-spot href="#case-2" data-go="case-2">
          <div class="cv">
            <div class="mk" aria-hidden="true">
              <div class="mk-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>payments — orchestration layer</span></div>
              <div class="mk-arch">
                <div class="mk-row prod"><span class="mk-node up">Internal teams</span><span class="mk-node up">Client apps</span></div>
                <div class="mk-core"><b>One API</b><span>SDK or direct API · routing</span><i class="mk-pulse"></i></div>
                <div class="mk-row nets"><span class="mk-node dn">Provider A</span><span class="mk-node dn">Provider B</span><span class="mk-node dn">Provider C</span><span class="mk-node dn ledg">Our own gateway</span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="ct"><span class="tag">Platform design</span>
            <h3>One API for every payment provider</h3>
            <p class="res"><span>Providers: <b>3 → 40+</b></span> · <span>Integrations per team: <b>1</b></span></p>
          </div>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
    {/* 5. How I build */}
    <section class="w sec" id="approach">
      <div class="sh">
        <h2 class="grad">How I build</h2>
        <p>Learned building payments with a small team. They hold for any product people have to trust.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="feat">
        <li><svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M10 3l7 4-7 4-7-4 7-4z"/><path d="M3 11l7 4 7-4"/></svg>
          <h3>Be your own customer</h3>
          <p>We ran our own wallet on the gateway, so we felt every rough edge before a client did.</p>
        </li>
        <li><svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M10 3v5m0 0l-5 5m5-5l5 5M5 13v4m10-4v4"/></svg>
          <h3>Settings, not sprints</h3>
          <p>New clients get onboarded, not built for. When sales asks “Can we…?” the answer should be a setting.</p>
        </li>
        <li><svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M2 10s3-5.5 8-5.5S18 10 18 10s-3 5.5-8 5.5S2 10 2 10z"/><circle cx="10" cy="10" r="2.5"/></svg>
          <h3>Controls are product</h3>
          <p>If a control is painful, people route around it, and then it isn’t a control.</p>
        </li>
        <li><svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="10" cy="10" r="7"/><circle cx="10" cy="10" r="3.5"/><circle cx="10" cy="10" r=".8"/></svg>
          <h3>Design the exits first</h3>
          <p>Refunds, rejections and failed transfers are where trust is won or lost. They get an owner before launch.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    {/* 8. Contact */}
    <section class="cta" id="contact">
      <div class="glow2" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="w">
        <h2 class="grad">Working on something worth building?</h2>
        <p>Let’s talk. Send a message, or find me on LinkedIn.</p>
        <div class="ctas c"><a class="btn" href="#" data-link="linkedin" data-cta="Opens Drake’s LinkedIn profile.">LinkedIn</a><button class="btn ghost" type="button" data-contact-open>Send a message</button></div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
  {/* ============ CASE STUDY 1 (FLAGSHIP): FROM A ONE-LINE BRIEF TO A PAYMENTS PLATFORM ============ */}
  {/* Told in three chapters, one per phase of the platform diagram. On wide, tall screens the live diagram is
       pinned beside the chapters and home.js rebuilds it as each one scrolls past; on narrow or short screens,
       and without JavaScript, each chapter shows its own still diagram instead. */}
  <article data-view="case-1" hidden class="case flag">
    <header class="w ch"><a class="back" href="#work" data-go="home" data-anchor="work">← What I’ve built</a><span class="tag">Build and scale · Payments platform, 2021–2026</span>
      <h1 class="grad">From a one-line brief to a payments platform</h1>
      <p class="lede">I designed an in-house payment gateway, hired the engineers and took it live in under a year, and payment costs fell 68%. Then we opened it to outside businesses and built a consumer wallet and a treasury product on top of it. The platform grew from two internal clients to around 20, and to more than US$1 billion a year in transaction volume.</p>
      <dl class="chips">
        <div>
          <dt>Role</dt>
          <dd>Senior Product Manager (Head of Blockchain)</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Team</dt>
          <dd>4 engineers I hired, growing to 14 people</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Duration</dt>
          <dd>2021–2026</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Company</dt>
          <dd>OG Global</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>
    <section class="w">
      <ul class="kpis">
        <li><b>68%</b><span>lower payment costs than with the vendors it replaced, measured over 2022, the first full year live</span><em>Phase 1</em></li>
        <li><b>2 → <span aria-hidden="true">~</span><span class="sr">about </span>20</b><span>clients on the platform</span><em>Phases 2–3</em></li>
        <li><b>US$1B+</b><span>a year in transaction volume at full scale</span><em>Phase 3</em></li>
        <li><b>23%</b><span>lower network fee per withdrawal</span><em>Phase 3</em></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <nav class="w fl-nav" aria-label="Chapters"><a href="#a1-zero"><span>Phase 1</span>Zero to one</a><a href="#a1-market"><span>Phase 2</span>Taking it to market</a><a href="#a1-scale"><span>Phase 3</span>One to 100</a></nav>
    <section class="w story fl-story" aria-label="The story, in three phases">
      <div class="st-grid">
        <div class="st-steps">
          <section class="st-step cs-sec fl-ch on" id="a1-zero" aria-labelledby="a1-zero-h">
            <p class="st-ph">Phase 1 · 2021–2022</p>
            <h2 id="a1-zero-h">Zero to one</h2>
            <p class="st-txt">I launched the gateway and its admin console in November 2021, both on one shared core.</p>
            <figure class="fl-still"><PlatformDiagram phase={1} /></figure>
            <p>Payments ran through third-party providers, and leadership asked whether we could build our own. The brief fit in a sentence: “Bring crypto payments in-house: lower cost, our own custody, our own compliance checks.”</p>
            <p>I sketched my answer at a company party and presented it. There was no product, no team and no code yet, just the sketch and the freedom to hire.</p>
            <p>I owned the product end to end, along with the division’s budget and vendor decisions, and reported to the CTO. I designed the solution, got buy-in from the stakeholders, hired the engineering team and finalized the design with them. I worked out the API design with our system architect, and the compliance rules with our compliance leadership.</p>
            <p>Two constraints shaped the architecture. We had four engineers, and we were handling other people’s money. Every movement needed screening, approvals and an audit trail.</p>
            <h3>Key decisions</h3>
            <div class="cs-decs">
              <div class="cs-dec"><span>01</span><b>Build the core once</b>
                <p>Custody, screening, the ledger and approvals lived in one shared core. Every product built later would use that core rather than fork it.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="cs-dec"><span>02</span><b>Separate funds by risk</b>
                <p>Clean, higher-risk and failed-screening funds had separate balances and wallets. The system enforced separation by design instead of relying on operators to get every decision right.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="cs-dec"><span>03</span><b>Make payment status explicit</b>
                <p>Clients received one callback when a payment was detected and another once it was confirmed, screened and credited. That gave customers timely feedback without letting client applications credit funds prematurely.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <figure class="art cs-art">
              <div class="mk" role="img" aria-label="An incoming deposit in the gateway: seen on the network, with the first callback sent; confirmed, with the sender's address screened and clean; checked against limits, with the fee applied; and credited to the clean balance, with the final callback.">
                <div class="mk-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>gateway — incoming deposit</span></div>
                <div class="mk-ap">
                  <div class="mk-head"><b>Deposit</b><span class="mk-chip">Two callbacks</span><span class="mk-fil">Every step logged</span></div>
                  <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">1</span>
                    <div><b>Seen on the network</b><span>First callback <i class="">Sent</i></span></div>
                    <span class="mk-btn sm">Sent</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">2</span>
                    <div><b>Confirmed</b><span>Sender address screened <i class="">Clean</i></span></div>
                    <span class="mk-btn sm">Pass</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">3</span>
                    <div><b>Limits and fee</b><span>Within limits <i class="">Fee applied</i></span></div>
                    <span class="mk-btn sm">Pass</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">4</span>
                    <div><b>Credited</b><span>Clean balance <i class="">Final callback</i></span></div>
                    <span class="mk-btn sm">Done</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <figcaption>An incoming deposit: two callbacks to the client, and the sender is screened before anything is credited.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <blockquote class="cs-pull">Separate balances beat careful people.</blockquote>
            <p>In 2022, the gateway’s first full year live, payment costs came in 68% lower than with the vendors it replaced, and the savings held in the years after.</p>
            <p class="fl-lesson"><b>Lesson</b>A small team forces good architecture. With four engineers, we couldn’t afford a separate codebase for every product, so we built one core that could support what came next.</p>
          </section>
          <section class="st-step cs-sec fl-ch" id="a1-market" aria-labelledby="a1-market-h">
            <p class="st-ph">Phase 2 · 2022–2024</p>
            <h2 id="a1-market-h">Taking it to market</h2>
            <p class="st-txt">We opened the gateway to outside businesses, then built a consumer wallet as one of its clients.</p>
            <figure class="fl-still"><PlatformDiagram phase={2} /></figure>
            <p>The gateway already served two internal clients. The infrastructure was in place, so the next question was whether it should remain an internal capability or become a product in its own right.</p>
            <p>In 2022, we chose to take it to market as a B2B platform. Outside clients meant new revenue and an opportunity to grow the team. Going to market needed the CTO, the COO and the rest of the leadership team to agree to and fund it, and sales to find the clients. I retained product ownership.</p>
            <div class="cs-trades">
              <div class="cs-trade"><b>New revenue from outside clients</b><span>over</span><s>A team focused only on internal needs</s>
                <p>What it cost:</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <ul class="cs-list">
              <li>The admin console, built for our own operators, had to be rebuilt as multi-tenant.</li>
              <li>The team’s time was split between our own needs and outside clients.</li>
              <li>Outside clients brought their own support and operations work.</li>
              <li>We took on compliance responsibility for other businesses’ customers.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The rebuilt admin console was one system shared by our operators and every client, and each client saw only its own transactions, users, reports and flagged payments. Roles and permissions controlled who could see and do what, and every action was logged, so an audit could show who did what and when. Clients could manage their own operations instead of relying on our support team for every task.</p>
            <h3>Key decisions</h3>
            <div class="cs-decs">
              <div class="cs-dec"><span>01</span><b>Configure, don’t fork</b>
                <p>Assets, payment methods, limits and fees became configurable at the platform, client and brand levels. Onboarding a new client meant configuring the platform, not maintaining another codebase.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="cs-dec"><span>02</span><b>Make our wallet a client of our own gateway</b>
                <p>Not every client wanted to build a product on top of our API; some wanted a turnkey solution. We built a white-label consumer wallet that connected to the gateway like any other client, using the same custody, screening and approval controls. Running the wallet ourselves also gave sales a working product to demonstrate.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>We expanded support to five blockchain networks and additional tokens. The wallet launched in early 2024, giving us a second way to take the platform to market: a turnkey product that could be sold and bundled alongside the API.</p>
            <p class="fl-lesson"><b>Lesson</b>Build for outside clients before you need to. An internal tool becomes a very different product when other businesses depend on it.</p>
          </section>
          <section class="st-step cs-sec fl-ch" id="a1-scale" aria-labelledby="a1-scale-h">
            <p class="st-ph">Phase 3 · 2024–2026</p>
            <h2 id="a1-scale-h">One to 100</h2>
            <p class="st-txt">The treasury product came next, and the platform grew to around 20 clients.</p>
            <figure class="fl-still"><PlatformDiagram phase={3} /></figure>
            <p>Our own treasury team, like several of our clients, needed a safer way to hold funds and make settlements and payouts. We built a treasury product around custody and controlled fund movements, and clients could also license it on its own.</p>
            <p>Every payout went through layered approvals, recording who approved it, when, why and for which business unit, so the books could be reconciled. Before an operator completed a payout or accepted a deposit, the treasury showed the screening result for the address, so they could judge the risk first. Higher-risk deposits could be isolated for review rather than mixed with clean funds.</p>
            <figure class="art cs-art">
              <div class="mk" role="img" aria-label="Treasury payouts and approvals: a supplier payout at approval 2 of 3, with its address screened; a client settlement under maker-checker, to a registered wallet; a customer withdrawal paid from a deposit address and routed; and a deposit from a higher-risk source, isolated for review.">
                <div class="mk-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>treasury — payouts and approvals</span></div>
                <div class="mk-ap">
                  <div class="mk-head"><b>Waiting for approval</b><span class="mk-chip">Up to 3 layers</span><span class="mk-fil">Every action logged</span></div>
                  <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">P</span>
                    <div><b>Supplier payout</b><span>Approval 2 of 3 <i class="">Address screened</i></span></div>
                    <span class="mk-btn sm">Approve</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">S</span>
                    <div><b>Client settlement</b><span>Maker-checker <i class="">Wallet registered</i></span></div>
                    <span class="mk-btn sm">Approve</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">W</span>
                    <div><b>Customer withdrawal</b><span>Paid from deposit address <i class="">Routed</i></span></div>
                    <span class="mk-btn sm">Sent</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">D</span>
                    <div><b>Incoming deposit</b><span>Higher-risk source <i class="warn">Isolated for review</i></span></div>
                    <span class="mk-btn sm">Review</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
              <figcaption>Payouts wait for up to three layers of approval, and every action is logged.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <h3>Network fees</h3>
            <p>TRON’s network voted its energy price up through 2021 and 2022, and that led us to build withdrawal routing into the gateway, which cut the network fee per withdrawal by 23%. Fees kept moving: from 2024, Ethereum’s fell as more activity moved to layer-2 networks, and TRON, once among our cheapest networks, ended up costing more than Ethereum.</p>
            <p>By 2026, we had spent five years building on the original core, extending the gateway, adding products and improving the transaction flows behind them. What started as a sketch became a platform that processed more than US$1 billion a year.</p>
            <p class="fl-lesson"><b>Lesson</b>Network fees are a product problem, because they don’t stay put.</p>
          </section>
        </div>
        <div class="st-pin"><PlatformDiagram phase={1} live /></div>
      </div>
    </section>
