  {/* ============ CASE STUDY 3: A TURNKEY WALLET, BUILT ON OUR OWN GATEWAY ============ */}
  {/* Rendered only while CASE_OPTIONS.walletCase is on (src/scripts/case-options.ts). Copy: _workbench/discovery/wallet/wallet-case-draft.md */}
  {CASE_OPTIONS.walletCase && (
  <article data-view="case-3" hidden class="case">
    <header class="w ch"><a class="back" href="#work" data-go="home" data-anchor="work">← What I’ve built</a><span class="tag">Customer-led · Custodial wallet, 2023 onward</span>
      <h1 class="grad">A turnkey wallet, built on our own gateway</h1>
      <p class="lede">Clients wanted a turnkey wallet, and more than one committed before we started building. We built it as a client of our own payment gateway, soft-launched it in early 2024, and about two months later had three clients running in production across three markets.</p>
      <dl class="chips">
        <div>
          <dt>Role</dt>
          <dd>Senior Product Manager (Head of Blockchain)</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Team</dt>
          <dd>Blockchain division</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Duration</dt>
          <dd>2023 onward</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Company</dt>
          <dd>OG Global</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>
    <section class="w">
      <ul class="kpis">
        <li><b>1 → 3</b><span>clients in production across three markets, about two months after launch</span><em>Adoption</em></li>
        <li><b>Instant</b><span>KYC, down from 24–48 hours with our manual process</span><em>Onboarding</em></li>
        <li><b><span aria-hidden="true">~</span><span class="sr">about </span>65%</b><span>saved by customers renting energy instead of burning TRX</span><em>Cost</em></li>
        <li><b><span aria-hidden="true">~</span><span class="sr">about </span>3.5</b><span>months from launch to the first major enhancement, a P2P exchange</span><em>Pace</em></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <div class="w cgrid">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <p>On this page</p>
        <a href="#a3-context">Context</a><a href="#a3-decision">The decision</a><a href="#a3-built">How it’s built</a><a href="#a3-challenges">Challenges and decisions</a><a href="#a3-shipped">How it shipped</a><a href="#a3-lessons">What I’d carry forward</a>
      </nav>
      <div class="prose">
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-context" data-sec>
          <h2>Context</h2>
          <p>Clients wanted a turnkey wallet. Our APIs already let someone build their own wallet with their own layer on top, using us for custody, deposits and withdrawals, transaction confirmation and screening, and KYC/KYB onboarding to stay compliant in the jurisdictions where they operated.</p>
          <p>In practice, people didn’t want to build this themselves; they wanted something ready-made. And the demand was concrete. More than one client committed before we started building. One business partner was using a vendor whose similar solution hadn’t met expectations, and wanted to replace it as soon as possible. That partner became our first launch client.</p>
          <p>I owned the wallet end to end, as I did every product on the platform: the roadmap, the design and the trade-offs.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-decision" data-sec>
          <h2>The decision</h2>
          <p>The first decision was whether to take on a new product line. The infrastructure already existed, the buy-in was there, and so was the customer base. The remaining question was whether the product would provide value and be something we could build on in the years to come.</p>
          <p>The costs were engineering time, plus time spent planning and gathering input from operations, legal and other stakeholders. Before moving forward, we needed:</p>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>the costing to be viable,</li>
            <li>total costs to be recouped in a reasonable time, and</li>
            <li>some form of guarantee from clients that they would use the product.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-built" data-sec>
          <h2>How it’s built</h2>
          <p>We became a client of our own payment gateway. The wallet ran on the same infrastructure and under the same controls we provided to other clients: custody, screening and approvals, KYC and compliance, limits, fees, and crypto and token availability. Fees, limits, token availability and similar settings were configurable per deployment or per tenant.</p>
          <h3>Onboarding: from 24–48 hours to instant</h3>
          <p>At launch, KYC used our own very manual process, which could take 24–48 hours to verify a user. About eight months after launch, we moved to a vendor that ran KYC and other compliance checks instantly; manual attention was needed only when the vendor raised a flag.</p>
          <p>To pick the vendor, I chose two to test, designed an A/B test against the manual flow and what counted as success, and picked the better one on the results. Compliance reviewed the vendor and signed off. With the vendor in place, 8% more of the people who started onboarding finished it.</p>
          <p>The vendor could handle multiple jurisdictions, so all tenants used the same vendor. Each tenant had its own workflow to satisfy its jurisdictional requirements, which kept data isolated and put every user through the correct KYC process for their jurisdiction.</p>
          <figure class="art cs-art">
            <div class="mk" role="img" aria-label="Wallet onboarding with a vendor: an identity check verified instantly, other compliance checks cleared instantly, a case flagged by the vendor waiting for a person to review it, and an account opened.">
              <div class="mk-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>wallet — onboarding with a vendor</span></div>
              <div class="mk-ap">
                <div class="mk-head"><b>New customer</b><span class="mk-chip">Vendor checks</span><span class="mk-fil">Was 24–48 hours</span></div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">1</span>
                  <div><b>Identity check (KYC)</b><span>Verified <i class="">Instant</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Done</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">2</span>
                  <div><b>Compliance checks</b><span>Cleared <i class="">Instant</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Done</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">3</span>
                  <div><b>Flagged case</b><span>Raised by the vendor <i class="warn">Needs a person</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Review</span>
                </div>
                <div class="mk-arow"><span class="mk-av lg">4</span>
                  <div><b>Account opened</b><span>Ready to use <i class="">Onboarded</i></span></div>
                  <span class="mk-btn sm">Done</span>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <figcaption>Checks ran instantly; a person stepped in only when the vendor raised a flag.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-challenges" data-sec>
          <h2>Challenges and decisions</h2>
          <h3>Customization</h3>
          <p>Multiple clients wanted the wallet, and each had their own opinions. We met each client’s most important demands by allowing some customization, but not so much that it expanded scope or made maintenance impossible. We didn’t want the product to become a collection of one client’s exceptions, so for each request I decided whether to support it, how, and with what priority. That meant a lot of configuration work.</p>
          <h3>Tenant data isolation and security</h3>
          <p>Clients operated in different jurisdictions, and so did their users. We had to store their data compliantly, prevent data breaches, and make sure no tenant could access data that wasn’t theirs.</p>
          <h3>Our costliest mistake: assuming multi-tenant was enough</h3>
          <p>Some jurisdictions required an isolated solution. The isolation wasn’t just per client; the jurisdiction itself needed its own tenant. We built a system that could replicate as many instances as needed, with:</p>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>data hosted in the correct location,</li>
            <li>data accessible only from the correct location, and</li>
            <li>everything feeding into an admin console segregated by tenant.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The system also had to be maintainable, deployable by DevOps and monitorable. A setup like this grows quickly, especially when a jurisdiction needs its own customizations, or a requirement has to go to all deployments or only some. We relied heavily on feature flags, and on deploying individual instances in Kubernetes pods in the right locations. This structural overhaul happened mid-project and cost about a month.</p>
          <h3>Web and mobile at the same time</h3>
          <p>We originally planned to go web first. Client data showed most usage came from mobile apps, so I moved us to launching web and mobile at the same time. The team had never published a mobile app. I had worked with mobile apps before and knew the process, but it was new for engineering, and for operations, who handled app deployments and dealt with Apple and Google.</p>
          <h3>Our first client: soft launch, P2P and migration</h3>
          <p>Our first launch client was the partner who wanted to replace their underperforming vendor. We started with a soft launch, running the client’s existing vendor wallet and our new wallet in parallel.</p>
          <p>There were too many features to launch everything at once, so the P2P exchange had to wait, and we negotiated with clients how the wallet would work without it at first. Our other clients wanted access to P2P. This client already had it through its previous vendor and considered it necessary for feature parity with that solution before it could fully roll off it, which put pressure on our team to deliver it quickly. We shipped P2P about 3.5 months after launch as our first major enhancement. We built a structured appeal flow into it: if a trade ran into a problem, the two users could open an appeal, submit evidence and work it out with each other in real time, before anything needed to go to our customer support team.</p>
          <p>Later, we migrated the client’s users from its previous vendor with an account migration system we built for it, and the client could end its contract with that vendor. Technically, the migration went smoothly. But the huge wave of customers arriving at once created an operations and customer support peak we weren’t prepared for. There were no serious technical issues, just a lot of people with questions.</p>
          <h3>The TRON energy pool</h3>
          <p>From launch, we shared the energy pool for our own TRON transactions with customers. They liked it for three reasons:</p>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>Renting energy saved them around 65% compared to burning TRX; they paid about a third of the TRX cost.</li>
            <li>We priced at or slightly below market rate.</li>
            <li>The differentiator: customers could pay for energy with any currency in their wallet, not just TRX. They could pay in a stablecoin without an extra transaction fee for sending that stablecoin.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The feature was more popular than we anticipated, and customer usage consumed the energy we needed for our own internal operations. We fixed it by staking more TRX to grow the pool. It was a relatively small challenge, but one we didn’t foresee; at the start, we thought we had energy to spare.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-shipped" data-sec>
          <h2>How it shipped</h2>
          <ol class="cs-steps">
            <li><span>2023</span>Project begins</li>
            <li><span>Early 2024</span>Soft launch with our first client brand, running in parallel with that client’s previous vendor wallet. The TRON energy pool is live from launch.</li>
            <li><span><span aria-hidden="true">~</span><span class="sr">about </span>2 months in</span>Two more clients onboarded: three clients running in production across three markets</li>
            <li><span><span aria-hidden="true">~</span><span class="sr">about </span>3.5 months in</span>The P2P exchange ships as the first major enhancement, about 1.5 months after the second wave</li>
            <li><span><span aria-hidden="true">~</span><span class="sr">about </span>8 months in</span>KYC moves from the manual process to a vendor</li>
            <li><span>Later</span>The first client’s users migrate from their previous vendor, after P2P and the KYC move</li>
          </ol>
        </section>
        <section class="cs-sec" id="a3-lessons" data-sec>
          <h2>What I’d carry forward</h2>
          <ul class="cs-list">
            <li>Plan for jurisdictions, not just clients. Finding out mid-project that some needed their own isolated instance cost us about a month.</li>
            <li>Plan the support for a migration, not just the code. Technically, it went smoothly; the wave of questions is what we weren’t ready for.</li>
            <li>Size shared resources for success. At the start, we thought we had energy to spare.</li>
            <li>Test vendors side by side on real users, not on their pitch.</li>
            <li>Bring compliance the data, not a request. The results made the approval easy.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        {CHAT_OPTIONS.enabled && (
        <aside class="askc" aria-labelledby="askc-3"><span class="ij-av lg" aria-hidden="true">DK</span>
          <div>
            <h2 id="askc-3">Ask Drake about this case</h2>
            <p>Pre-written answers from the interview room, with the evidence attached.</p>
            <div class="askc-chips"><button class="ij-chip" type="button" data-ask="zero">Have you taken a product from zero to one?</button><button class="ij-chip" type="button" data-ask="measure">How do you measure success?</button></div>
          </div>
        </aside>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
